1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,084
We are team NgaChainAILayer2.

2
00:00:02,134 --> 00:00:07,883
In Round 1 we found that late deliveries at Company A start as exceptions nobody

3
00:00:07,933 --> 00:00:10,664
owns in time, and proposed PromiseOps:

4
00:00:10,714 --> 00:00:16,392
catch orders that are about to miss their original promise early enough to save

5
00:00:16,442 --> 00:00:20,107
them, give each one an owner, and prove the result.

6
00:00:20,157 --> 00:00:21,882
This is the working MVP.

7
00:00:21,932 --> 00:00:24,950
It runs locally and all data is fictional.

8
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:27,278
It is 09:00 at DC Stockholm South.

9
00:00:27,328 --> 00:00:31,215
Every order is a course of four controls on one timetable:

10
00:00:31,265 --> 00:00:35,352
pick, dispatch cutoff, last viable dispatch, and the promise.

11
00:00:35,402 --> 00:00:36,742
The red line is now.

12
00:00:36,792 --> 00:00:40,076
The yellow bar at the bottom is the demo harness:

13
00:00:40,126 --> 00:00:44,950
it stands in for the real order and warehouse systems and for the clock.

14
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,712
At 09:30 two orders missed their pick.

15
00:00:48,762 --> 00:00:53,354
SE-1042 is the order from our Round 1 proposal:

16
00:00:53,404 --> 00:01:00,829
a B2B site delivery of plasterboard, promised for 14:00, on the 10:00 route.

17
00:01:00,879 --> 00:01:04,885
It ties with SE-1051, and B2B goes first.

18
00:01:04,935 --> 00:01:11,578
The rule only fires because the warehouse feed was read after 09:30:

19
00:01:11,628 --> 00:01:14,950
silence is never treated as proof.

20
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:25,254
Anna, the coordinator, takes ownership, which stops the fifteen-minute escalation

21
00:01:25,304 --> 00:01:26,064
timer.

22
00:01:26,114 --> 00:01:29,405
The goods were not picked.

23
00:01:29,455 --> 00:01:38,823
An expedite spends warehouse capacity, so a coordinator cannot approve it:

24
00:01:38,873 --> 00:01:44,950
she escalates to the shift lead with the reason.

25
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:53,546
Erik, the shift lead, moves a picker and records the expedite with evidence and its

26
00:01:53,596 --> 00:01:55,141
cost, ten euro.

27
00:01:55,191 --> 00:02:03,634
Round 1 promised that the cost of every action is logged, so Finance can check our

28
00:02:03,684 --> 00:02:06,258
business-case assumption.

29
00:02:06,308 --> 00:02:14,958
The activity log shows who took the case, who escalated, who decided, why, and when.

30
00:02:15,008 --> 00:02:19,950
Nothing is written back to the warehouse system.

31
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:27,006
SE-1051 was packed at 09:20; the handheld synced late.

32
00:02:27,056 --> 00:02:33,024
Anna closes it as a false alarm with evidence.

33
00:02:33,074 --> 00:02:39,950
That number tells us later how precise the rules are.

34
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:45,079
What if the warehouse feed goes quiet?

35
00:02:45,129 --> 00:02:56,356
After thirty minutes the feed turns red, a data task opens for the integration desk,

36
00:02:56,406 --> 00:03:00,282
and no new alerts are raised.

37
00:03:00,332 --> 00:03:11,024
Controls that passed while the feed was silent are shown as unknown, not missed:

38
00:03:11,074 --> 00:03:14,950
an empty feed proves nothing.

39
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:24,678
When the feed recovers, the backlog arrives first and only then do the rules run.

40
00:03:24,728 --> 00:03:27,715
They find a real problem:

41
00:03:27,765 --> 00:03:33,619
SE-1077 is picked but has no vehicle until 11:50.

42
00:03:33,669 --> 00:03:43,586
The promise cannot be kept, so Anna hands it to customer service, who give the site

43
00:03:43,636 --> 00:03:44,950
a new time.

44
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:47,057
Five hours later.

45
00:03:47,107 --> 00:03:56,664
SE-1042 was picked and dispatched late, shown in brown, but delivered at 12:32,

46
00:03:56,714 --> 00:03:59,738
before its 14:00 promise.

47
00:03:59,788 --> 00:04:09,950
SE-1077 arrived at 14:35 and is counted late, even though the customer was informed.

48
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:16,895
Outcomes are measured against the original promise, which can never be edited.

49
00:04:16,945 --> 00:04:24,370
An order counts as late as soon as its promise passes without a delivery, so silence

50
00:04:24,420 --> 00:04:26,629
never looks like success.

51
00:04:26,679 --> 00:04:29,508
One late delivery was prevented.

52
00:04:29,558 --> 00:04:36,364
At the Round 1 assumption of twenty-five euro per late delivery that is worth

53
00:04:36,414 --> 00:04:40,834
twenty-five euro, next to ten euro of logged cost.

54
00:04:40,884 --> 00:04:48,044
The scorecard uses the Round 1 pilot targets, and says honestly that three alerts

55
00:04:48,094 --> 00:04:49,950
are too few to judge.

56
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:54,135
Now the same morning when nobody acts.

57
00:04:54,185 --> 00:04:59,625
SE-1042 is picked at 11:20 and delivered at 14:25:

58
00:04:59,675 --> 00:05:00,219
late.

59
00:05:00,269 --> 00:05:04,622
On-time falls from 93.3 to 86.7 percent.

60
00:05:04,672 --> 00:05:13,268
That difference is the value PromiseOps is meant to prove in the pilot, against

61
00:05:13,318 --> 00:05:14,950
control stores.

62
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:24,950
The interface also runs in Vietnamese, including system messages.

63
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:27,553
What changed since Round 1, and why.

64
00:05:27,603 --> 00:05:29,943
The scope is the one we proposed:

65
00:05:29,993 --> 00:05:35,666
one DC, two rules, one owner, escalation, outcomes against the original promise,

66
00:05:35,716 --> 00:05:37,986
read only, no AI in the product.

67
00:05:38,036 --> 00:05:43,567
We alert at the milestone instead of after a fifteen-minute grace, because the

68
00:05:43,617 --> 00:05:49,574
read-after-milestone rule already prevents false alarms and leaves more time to act;

69
00:05:49,624 --> 00:05:52,461
the grace stays a setting for the pilot.

70
00:05:52,511 --> 00:05:58,113
A demo harness replaces Company A's feeds and clock, and a role switch replaces

71
00:05:58,163 --> 00:05:59,227
single sign-on.

72
00:05:59,277 --> 00:06:04,950
We added the timetable view, Vietnamese, cost logging and the Round 1 scorecard.

73
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:06,161
Under the hood:

74
00:06:06,211 --> 00:06:12,406
one FastAPI service with clear modules, SQLite, idempotent ingestion through the

75
00:06:12,456 --> 00:06:18,340
same adapter for polled and pushed data, an API key for integrations, and 38

76
00:06:18,390 --> 00:06:22,881
automated tests, including a browser test of this journey.

77
00:06:22,931 --> 00:06:29,126
Every closed case is a labelled example for the risk model planned after Gate 2.

78
00:06:29,176 --> 00:06:29,950
Thank you.
